items.readme_excerpt:
Packet communication over bidirectional LEDs, inspired by Packet Radio. There is no photodiode: each LED emits light to transmit, and is used as a light sensor to receive. - The same LED sends and receives, so a device can talk through the status LED it already has. - Packets are checked, acknowledged and retransmitted when needed, and duplicates are dropped. - The receiver adapts to room light, to lamps being switched on and off and to mains flicker. - configuring a device by holding another one against it: WiFi credentials, keys, settings;
- reading data out of a device without opening it or adding a connector;
- a service or debug port through an existing indicator LED;
- learning and experimenting with optical communication. - packets of 0 to 64 bytes, text or binary;
- a CRC-16/X.25 checksum on every frame (the same FCS as AX.25);
- acknowledgements, up to 3 attempts per packet, and duplicate filtering;
- listening before transmitting, and a random pause before each retry, so that two boards sending at the same time sort themselves out. While receiving, the anode is left floating, and long wires pick up mains hum. The resistor sets the LED current while transmitting. The resistor plays no part in receiving.
